A||||7||88Style||Clutch squeel!!!!||||1||
Z||000000||88Style||10-14-2001||03:43 PM||||Well I installed a new clutch in my 88 Formula and when I try to start it up, it has the god awful high pitched squeel. It's one of the Fiero Store high puck style clutches. I thought about the possibility of installing it backwards but after reviewing the instructions, I don't see anything that says there is a forward or backward way of installing it (flip flopped). this clutch has no springs. There is enough pressure on what ever is making the noise to keep the engine from staying started. Does anyone have any ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||California Kid||10-14-2001||03:50 PM||||Sure sounds like you have to disk in with the wrong surface facing the flywheel. Everyone I've seen is only meant to got in one way.||||reg||1
Z||000002||88Style||10-14-2001||04:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B>Sure sounds like you have to disk in with the wrong surface facing the flywheel. Everyone I've seen is only meant to got in one way.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I thought about that too, but it is a kevlar clutch and both sides look the same physically. In addition it has the same sound with the clutch engaged as well as disengaged. The only thing I can think of is that the clutch is has a sort of offset. I forget the proper name for it but the part of the clutch that slides over the input shaft sticks out more on one side than the other. But it would seem to me that it would only squeel when engaged or atleast it would squeel worse when engaged. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||10-14-2001||05:23 PM||||How about with the clutch out and the tranny in neutral?||||reg||1
Z||000004||88Style||10-14-2001||05:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Phil:<BR><B>How about with the clutch out and the tranny in neutral?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>With the clutch pedal out, clutch engaged, and the transmission in neutral, it makes the same sound. I only ran the car for a few seconds with some turning over in an attempt to locate the noise. It was not driven. It has been several months sinse the car has been started but it fired right up with starting fluid. That is what leads me to believe that whatever is causing the noise is putting enough load on the engine to prevent it from idling. It's a horrible high pitched metal to metal sound. <P>Is there any adjustment on these clutches. Perhaps I have it installed too tight? It has a brand new pressure plate and disc. I did not resurface the flywheel because the flywheel looked good (according to the Haynes manual description?). Please excuse my ignorance of Fieros, so far this has a beast of a much different nature compared to any of the old Camaros or Vettes I've owned. I hope nothing is fried. I have evan taken the steps to remove the belts incase it was something else like an alternator bearing..<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||California Kid||10-14-2001||09:03 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88Style:<BR><B> I forget the proper name for it but the part of the clutch that slides over the input shaft sticks out more on one side than the other. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>The above is what I was referring to, the hub surface that protrudes more on one side that the other must be positioned away from the flywheel. Get someone is to verify this, but it seems to me that if you have the long hub at the flywheel, it's grinding against it, and you may have ruined the disk.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||88Style||10-14-2001||09:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B>The above is what I was referring to, the hub surface that protrudes more on one side that the other must be positioned away from the flywheel. Get someone is to verify this, but it seems to me that if you have the long hub at the flywheel, it's grinding against it, and you may have ruined the disk.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I had thought of that but wouldn't the clutch disc push away from the flywheel atleast enough to take the load off the engine? Maybe I have a combonation of issues with car not wanting to stay started and improperly installed clutch. If all that is wrong is my ignorance installing a new clutch and it costs me a new clutch, I will count myself lucky! I hope I didn't ruin the flywheel. I did loosen the dust cover a the bottom and tryed to stick my finger in it to see if there is any metal shavings but I didn't find anything. That being said, I could only just get my pinky between the cover and the bell housing and then not at the bottom. I guess I am going to be pulling the cradle back out of the car this weekend. One trick I have found about this car is that you can take the whole damn thing apart with a 13mm, 15mm, 18mm wrench and sockets. Also I have a 20in long 3/8in extension and a 14in 1/4in extention. These tools make life easier!<P>Now that I have something to go on, does anyone have any other suggestions to check?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||88Style||10-15-2001||09:55 AM||||I hate bumping but I wanted to gather as much info as possible before I tear it back apart. I've already read the achives as best as my searches will turn up..||||reg||1
